cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Sediment: How Streak Mechanics Drive Compelled Production of Low-Quality Digital Content"
message: "If you cite this concept note, please use the metadata below. A peer-reviewed version may appear later; check the related Zenodo deposit for the canonical citation once minted."
# Top-level type stays at the CFF 1.2.0 default (software) because the
# enum is {software, dataset} — no preprint/article slot exists at this
# level. The `preferred-citation` block below carries the actual
# article-typed citation that indexers should pick up.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yoon
    given-names: Jiyeon
abstract: |
  Concept note proposing 'sediment' as a category of human-generated,
  platform-compelled, demand-independent low-quality content, distinct
  from spam and AI slop. Models the lifecycle as Deposition then
  Compaction then Fossilization under streak mechanics, develops three
  competing psychological hypotheses (loss aversion, identity
  consistency, escalating commitment), and proposes four studies
  anchored by the GitHub 2016 streak-counter removal as a natural
  experiment.
keywords:
  - streak mechanics
  - gamification
  - content quality
  - Goodhart's Law
  - loss aversion
  - digital platforms
  - compelled production
license: CC-BY-4.0
version: 0.1.2
date-released: 2026-05-21
repository-code: "https://github.com/heznpc/sediment"

# The canonical citation: the concept-note preprint itself. CFF
# preferred-citation supports type: article, which downstream indexers
# (Zotero, Scholar) honor over the top-level type.
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: "Sediment: How Streak Mechanics Drive Compelled Production of Low-Quality Digital Content"
  authors:
    - family-names: Yoon
      given-names: Jiyeon
  year: 2026
  url: "https://github.com/heznpc/sediment"
  license: CC-BY-4.0
  abstract: >
    Streak mechanics compel users to produce filler content on days when
    they have nothing meaningful to contribute, simply to avoid losing
    an accumulated record. This concept note introduces 'sediment' as a
    category distinct from spam and AI slop, develops a three-stage
    lifecycle (Deposition, Compaction, Fossilization), and proposes
    four studies anchored by the GitHub 2016 streak-counter removal.
